<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Debug builds only.

  Android 9 and later block cleartext HTTP by default, so a debug build talking
  to a development backend over http:// fails every request before it leaves
  the device. The failure is silent and misleading: Dio reports a connection
  error, which the app maps to `NoInternetException`, so a blocked request
  looks identical to a phone with no signal.

  This used to list three hosts by name — localhost, 127.0.0.1 and 10.0.2.2 —
  which covered the emulator and an `adb reverse` tunnel but nothing else. A
  physical phone reaching this machine over Wi-Fi uses a private LAN address
  instead, and that address changes with the network, so a fixed list is wrong
  again as soon as someone joins a different Wi-Fi.

  Debug builds are never shipped, so permitting cleartext for the whole debug
  variant is the honest trade: it removes a recurring, hard-to-read failure at
  no cost to production traffic.

  Release builds never see this file. `usesCleartextTraffic` stays absent from
  the main manifest, so shipped traffic remains HTTPS-only.
-->
<network-security-config>
    <base-config cleartextTrafficPermitted="true" />
</network-security-config>
